The scientist uses 50 mL of the 10% solution and 150 mL of the 30% solution."}}]},"exerciseNumber":2}]}]}],"digests":[{"topic":"break-even-analysis","title":"Break-even Analysis","blocks":[{"id":"p12-definition","text":"Break-even analysis - **Break-even analysis** is an application of simultaneous equations where one equation is **cost**, and the other is **revenue**. - The **break-even point** occurs when costs match income: - You make no profit or loss. - For the models shown, to the left of this point, you oper"},{"id":"p12-paired-example","text":"Solve problems using simultaneous linear models **Worked example** **Your turn** Worked example graph Square coordinate grid with numbered x ticks 20–100 and y ticks 100–400; blue revenue and red cost lines continue with arrows. Minor grid steps are 5 hot dogs and 20 dollars. R=4n; C=150+n; intersec"},{"id":"p13-break-even-theory","text":"Break-even analysis using simultaneous equations For a revenue equation $R$ and cost equation $C$: **Find the break-even point:** Find $n$ when: $$\text{Revenue}=\text{Cost}$$ **Find profit/loss** $$\text{Profit/Loss}=\text{Revenue}-\text{Cost}$$ **Profit** is when the difference is positive, **loss"},{"id":"p13-muffin-example-block","text":"Solve problems using simultaneous linear models Raf’s Muffin Shop has a fixed cost of \$320 per day and a variable production cost of \$2 per muffin. The selling price of each muffin is \$6. Write an equation for the cost, $C$, of producing $n$ muffins. $$C=320+2n$$ Worked example, graph at right Pe"},{"id":"p14-guided-practice-block","text":"Guided practice Jenny’s Sandwich Shop had a fixed expense of $\$1500$ per month and a cost of $\$1.50$ per sandwich. The sandwiches were sold for $\$3.50$ each. Write linear equations to model the production cost $C$ and revenue $R$ for $n$ sandwiches. $C=$[[$1500+1.5n$|26]] $R=$[[$3.5n$|15]] Graph "}]},{"topic":"quadratic-modelling","title":"Quadratic Modelling","blocks":[{"id":"p15-theory-block","text":"Quadratic modelling key-ideas Quadratic modelling Many scenarios can be modelled and solved using a quadratic equation. 1. Let a variable represent the unknown number. 2. Write an equation to describe the problem. (A diagram is helpful.) 3. Solve the equation. 4. Check your answer makes sense and ha"},{"id":"p15-example-block","text":"Model and solve problems using quadratic equations The area of a rectangle is fixed at $28\text{ m}^2$ and its length is $3\text{ m}$ more than its breadth. Find the width of the rectangle. 1. Let $x$ be the width. 2. $x(x+3)=28$ 3. $$\begin{aligned}x^2+3x&=28\\x^2+3x-28&=0\\(x+7)(x-4)&=0\\x&=-7\tex"},{"id":"p15-guided-block","text":"Guided practice A rectangle has area of $40\text{ m}^2$. Its length is $3\text{ m}$ longer than its breadth. Find the length of the rectangle. A triangle has area of $24\text{ m}^2$. Its height is $2\text{ m}$ more than its base. Calculate the length of the base. A rectangular garden, $10\text{ m}$ "}]}],"outline":[{"topic":"linear-modelling","id":"p4-q1","text":"Identify what the gradient and $y$-intercepts of these graphs represent, then write the equation. question 1a Unnumbered, unticked axes with arrowheads and an increasing blue straight line with positive vertical intercept; no grid. C = 0.5t + 2. The unnumbered source graph is schematic; independent "}]}
